\subsection{Related Work and Research Gaps}
Prior research on substation cybersecurity falls into five main categories:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{GOOSE Protocol Exploits:} Schuster \textit{et al.}~\cite{schuster2017goose} and Hoyos \textit{et al.}~\cite{hoyos2012real} demonstrated that unauthenticated GOOSE frames can be spoofed to trip circuit breakers. Yang \textit{et al.}~\cite{yang2013cyber} mapped the cyber-attack surface of IEC~61850 substations. These works established the necessity of authentication but did not evaluate authentication overhead as an attack surface.
    \item \textbf{IEC 62351-6 Performance Overhead:} Hussain \textit{et al.}~\cite{hussain2019goose} and Kabir \textit{et al.}~\cite{kabir2020goose} evaluated RSA and ECDSA computational costs on microcontrollers, noting that cryptographic delays can approach or exceed real-time budgets. Ali \textit{et al.}~\cite{ali2020latency} measured digital signature verification delays. However, these studies evaluated only benign steady-state operation, overlooking adversarial exploitation.
    \item \textbf{Smart Grid Denial of Service:} Parviz \textit{et al.}~\cite{parviz2022dos} and Khan \textit{et al.}~\cite{khan2021survey} surveyed network-layer flooding in smart grids. Traditional DoS analysis focuses on network bandwidth exhaustion, whereas CIL-DoS targets CPU exhaustion via cryptographic verification. While signature verification DoS has been investigated in vehicular ad-hoc networks (V2X)~\cite{whyte2013security}, digital substations exhibit fundamentally different physical constraints: sub-3\,ms hard real-time deadlines, pre-engineered static bay topologies, and unrouted Layer~2 multicast buses.
    \item \textbf{Substation Intrusion Detection:} Quincozes \textit{et al.}~\cite{quincozes2021survey} surveyed IDS architectures for substations. While network IDSes detect anomalous packet rates, passive detection does not prevent verification queues from filling in real time.
    \item \textbf{Post-Quantum Smart Grid Security:} Recent studies explore quantum-safe transitions for smart grids. However, empirical timing evaluations of NIST FIPS 204/205 under IEC~61850 real-time constraints have remained absent from the literature.
\end{enumerate}

Table~\ref{tab:related_work} contrasts our work with prior research, highlighting our contribution in connecting cryptographic queue saturation with cyber-physical grid stability.

