%%  SECTION III: THREAT MODEL & CYBER-PHYSICAL IMPACT
%% ================================================================
\section{Threat Model and Cyber-Physical Impact}
\label{sec:threat_model}

\subsection{Network Model and Attacker Capabilities}
We consider a digital substation process bus connecting merging units, protection relays, and circuit breaker controllers via Layer~2 Ethernet. An adversary gains Layer~2 process bus access via an infected engineering workstation, compromised gateway, or physical Ethernet tap~\cite{cherepanov2017industroyer}. The attacker cannot forge valid private keys, but can inject arbitrary Layer~2 Ethernet frames at line rate.

Standard process buses operate on 100BASE-FX (100\,Mbps) or 1000BASE-SX (1\,Gbps) optical Ethernet. For typical 350-byte GOOSE frames, 100\,Mbps link capacity corresponds to $\approx 35{,}700$\,pps, and 1\,Gbps corresponds to $\approx 357{,}000$\,pps. Importantly, CIL-DoS does \emph{not} require saturating the physical network bandwidth: on embedded relays ($\mu = 1{,}745$\,fps), an attack rate of just 10,000\,pps consumes only $\approx 28$\,Mbps ($<28\%$ of 100\,Mbps). The network link remains uncongested, yet the cryptographic queue saturates, inducing severe tripping delays.

\subsection{CIL-DoS Attack Dynamics}
The attacker transmits syntactically compliant GOOSE frames with pseudo-random signature bytes at rate $\lambda_{\text{atk}}$. Because the receiver cannot distinguish valid signatures from invalid signatures prior to full cryptographic execution, every frame enters the verification queue. When $\lambda_{\text{atk}} > \mu$ (verifier service rate), the queue accumulates monotonically, and legitimate trip frames experience severe queueing delay $W_q$.

\subsection{Cyber-Physical Impact on Power Grid Stability}
\label{sec:cyber_physical}
In power transmission grids, a GOOSE trip delay is not merely a communication delay; it directly cascades into electromechanical instability and severe protection selectivity loss:

\subsubsection{Rotor Angle Stability and Critical Clearing Time (CCT)}
The transient electromechanical swing equation of a synchronous generator during a severe three-phase bus fault is governed by~\cite{kundur1994power}:
\begin{equation}
\frac{2H}{\omega_s} \frac{d^2 \delta(t)}{dt^2} = P_m - P_e(\delta) = P_m - P_{\max}^{\text{fault}} \sin(\delta(t)),
\label{eq:swing}
\end{equation}
where $H$ is the generator inertia constant ($3\text{--}5$\,s), $\omega_s = 2\pi f_0 = 120\pi$\,rad/s, $\delta(t)$ is the rotor electrical angle, $P_m$ is mechanical shaft power, and $P_e$ is electrical power transfer. During a bolted three-phase fault near the substation, $P_{\max}^{\text{fault}} \approx 0$, yielding accelerating power $P_a \approx P_m$. Integrating~\eqref{eq:swing} with initial rotor angle $\delta_0$ yields:
\begin{equation}
\delta(t) = \delta_0 + \frac{\omega_s P_m}{4H} t^2.
\end{equation}
The Critical Clearing Time (CCT, $t_{\text{crit}}$) defines the maximum allowable fault duration before $\delta > \delta_{\text{cr}}$, beyond which synchronism with the infinite bus is irreversibly lost.

\textit{SMIB Benchmark Case Study:} Consider a standard Single-Machine Infinite-Bus (SMIB) benchmark system~\cite{kundur1994power} with generator parameters: $H = 4.0$\,s, transient reactance $X_d' = 0.25$\,p.u., mechanical input $P_m = 0.8$\,p.u., and nominal operating angle $\delta_0 = 32.5^\circ = 0.5672$\,rad, giving pre-fault power transfer capability $P_{\max} = P_m / \sin \delta_0 = 1.4888$\,p.u. For a bolted fault ($P_e = 0$) cleared by restoring the line without topology change, the Equal-Area Criterion equates accelerating area $A_{\text{acc}} = P_m(\delta_{\text{cr}} - \delta_0)$ and decelerating area $A_{\text{dec}} = \int_{\delta_{\text{cr}}}^{\pi - \delta_0} (P_{\max}\sin\delta - P_m)d\delta$, yielding:
\begin{equation}
\cos \delta_{\text{cr}} = \sin \delta_0 (\pi - 2\delta_0) - \cos \delta_0.
\label{eq:eac}
\end{equation}
Substituting $\delta_0 = 32.5^\circ$:
\begin{equation}
\cos \delta_{\text{cr}} = 0.5373(2.0071) - 0.8434 = 0.2350,
\end{equation}
yielding critical clearing angle $\delta_{\text{cr}} = \arccos(0.2350) \approx 76.4^\circ$. The allowable angular advance is $\Delta \delta = 76.4^\circ - 32.5^\circ = 43.9^\circ$. The rotor acceleration in degrees is:
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
\delta(t) &= 32.5^\circ + \left( \frac{180}{\pi} \frac{2\pi \times 60 \times 0.8}{4 \times 4.0} \right) t^2 \\
&= 32.5^\circ + 1{,}080\,t^2 \text{ [deg]}.
\end{split}
\end{equation}
The corresponding Critical Clearing Time is:
\begin{equation}
t_{\text{crit}} = \sqrt{\frac{76.4^\circ - 32.5^\circ}{1{,}080}} = \sqrt{0.04065} \approx 201.6\,\text{ms} \approx 200\,\text{ms}.
\end{equation}
In stressed, heavily loaded, or low-inertia inverter-based grids ($H = 2.5$\,s, $P_m = 0.95$\,p.u., acceleration $\alpha = 2{,}052^\circ/\text{s}^2$, $\delta_{\text{cr}} = 69.4^\circ$), $t_{\text{crit}}$ compresses to $\approx 120$\,ms.
Under nominal primary protection, total clearance time is $t_{\text{clear}} = t_{\text{relay}} + t_{\text{GOOSE}} + t_{\text{mech}} \approx 15 + 1.2 + 40 = 56.2$\,ms, which operates safely well below $t_{\text{crit}}$.

\subsubsection{Breaker Failure (ANSI 50BF) and Loss of Protection Selectivity}
In high-voltage substations, an unmitigated CIL-DoS attack delaying primary GOOSE trip delivery beyond 2\,s triggers severe \emph{backup protection escalation}:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{ANSI 50BF Escalation on Flooded Bus:} Concurrently with primary trip initiation, relays start an ANSI 50BF Breaker Failure timer ($t_{\text{50BF}} \approx 150\text{--}200$\,ms). Because the primary breaker never receives the queued GOOSE trip, fault current persists past $t_{\text{50BF}}$. Crucially, in digital substations, ANSI 50BF backup schemes also communicate via GOOSE over the same process bus to trip adjacent breakers. Because the process bus is saturated by CIL-DoS traffic, backup 50BF GOOSE trip frames are \emph{also} delayed in subscriber queues.
    \item \textbf{Remote Zone 2 Escalation:} With both primary and local breaker failure tripping stalled by communication queueing, the uncleared fault persists until remote transmission distance relays detect it in Zone~2 ($t_{\text{Z2}} \approx 300\text{--}400$\,ms), tripping upstream substations.
\end{enumerate}
At 350\,ms backup clearance, the rotor angle reaches $\delta(0.35) = 32.5^\circ + 1{,}080(0.35)^2 = 164.8^\circ > \delta_{\text{cr}} = 76.4^\circ$. In fact, $\delta$ exceeds the decelerating limit ($\delta_{\max} = 147.5^\circ$), forcing the generator into irreversible pole slipping before Zone~2 clears, tripping ANSI 78 out-of-step relays and precipitating wide-area cascading outages.

\subsubsection{Thermal Stress and Equipment Degradation ($I^2 t$)}
Adiabatic thermal energy dissipated in high-voltage transformer windings and switchgear scales directly with fault duration. IEEE Std C57.109-2018~\cite{ieeec57109} establishes through-fault withstand limits for Category IV substation transformers ($>30$\,MVA) governed by $I^2 t = K_{\text{trans}}$ ($t = 1250/I_{\text{sc}}^2$ in p.u.):
\begin{equation}
E_{\text{thermal}} = \int_0^{t_{\text{clear}}} I_{\text{sc}}^2(t) \, dt \approx I_{\text{sc}}^2 t_{\text{clear}}.
\end{equation}
For a symmetrical $I_{\text{sc}} = 40$\,kA transmission bus fault:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{Nominal Clearance ($t_{\text{clear}} = 56$\,ms):} $E_{\text{nom}} = (40\,\text{kA})^2 \times 0.056\,\text{s} = 89.6\,\text{MA}^2\text{s}$.
    \item \textbf{Backup Clearance ($t_{\text{clear}} = 350$\,ms):} $E_{\text{backup}} = (40\,\text{kA})^2 \times 0.350\,\text{s} = 560.0\,\text{MA}^2\text{s}$.
\end{itemize}
This represents a \textbf{6.25$\times$ surge in thermal energy dissipation} ($3.6\times$ under 200\,ms 50BF clearance). Extended fault currents subject transformer windings to severe 120\,Hz pulsating electrodynamic Lorentz forces ($F \propto I_{\text{sc}}^2$), directly encroaching upon IEEE Std C57.109 mechanical damage boundaries.

%% ================================================================
%%  SECTION IV: SUBSCRIBER ARCHITECTURE & QUEUEING MODEL
%% ================================================================
\section{Subscriber Architecture and Queueing Theory}
\label{sec:architecture_queueing}

\subsection{Two-Thread Subscriber Implementation}
To decouple wire-speed frame reception from serialized verification, we implement a two-thread C subscriber:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{Receiver Thread (T1):} Reads raw Ethernet frames via raw sockets (\texttt{AF\_PACKET}, \texttt{SO\_RCVBUF = 16MB}), timestamps arrival, and executes admission triage in $<1\,\mu$s.
    \item \textbf{Verification Worker(s) (T2):} Dequeues frames, verifies signatures via OpenSSL/liboqs, updates state counters, and executes tripping logic.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Queueing Formulation and Saturation Analysis}
We model the verification queue as an $M/M/1$ (and $M/D/1$) system. Let $\lambda$ be total arrival rate and $\mu$ be cryptographic service rate ($\mu = 1/\overline{S}$). When utilization $\rho = \lambda/\mu < 1$, mean total latency $W$ is:
\begin{equation}
W = \frac{1}{\mu - \lambda} = \frac{\overline{S}}{1 - \rho}.
\end{equation}
The probability of violating the deadline $D = 3$\,ms is:
\begin{equation}
P(W > D) = \exp\left(-\mu(1 - \rho) D\right).
\end{equation}

When cryptographic execution is strictly deterministic (constant-time modular exponentiation), the Pollaczek-Khinchine formula for an $M/D/1$ queue yields:
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{E}[W_q^{M/D/1}] = \frac{\rho}{2\mu(1-\rho)} = \frac{1}{2} \mathbb{E}[W_q^{M/M/1}].
\end{equation}
The deterministic queueing delay is exactly half the exponential delay, confirming that the $M/M/1$ formulation serves as a conservative upper bound for safety auditing.

For a finite queue of capacity $K$ ($M/M/1/K$), when $\lambda \gg \mu$, the queue fills to capacity $K$. Mean waiting time under saturation becomes:
\begin{equation}
W_{\text{sat}} \approx \frac{K}{\mu} = K \cdot \overline{S}.
\label{eq:sat_wait}
\end{equation}
Eq.~\eqref{eq:sat_wait} reveals the fatal flaw of buffering: latency scales strictly with queue depth $K$. Expanding $K$ inflates queueing delay without increasing throughput.

\subsection{Multi-Worker Service Rate and Scaling Limits}
With $N$ parallel verification threads, aggregate service capacity is $\mu_N = N \cdot \mu$. Queue utilization under flood intensity $\lambda_{\text{atk}}$ is:
\begin{equation}
\rho_N = \frac{\lambda_{\text{atk}}}{N \cdot \mu}.
\end{equation}
To evaluate CIL-DoS across operational environments, our study examines two complementary tiers:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{Substation Embedded Relay Profile:} Calibrated to industrial protective relays (ARM Cortex-A9 @ 800\,MHz, raw verify $566\,\mu$s, full pipeline $\overline{S} = 573\,\mu$s, $\mu = 1{,}745$\,fps). Here, a 100K\,pps attack overwhelms a single worker ($\rho_1 = 57.3 \gg 1$). Deploying 4 parallel workers increases throughput to $6{,}980$\,fps, but utilization remains $\rho_4 = 14.3 \gg 1$, yielding 579\,ms queueing delay and \textbf{100\% deadline failure}. Surviving 100K\,pps on this relay core would require $N_{\text{required}} = 100{,}000 / 1{,}745 \approx 58$ dedicated cores.
    \item \textbf{High-Speed Evaluation Host Platform:} Our measured testbed (Apple M2 @ 3.49\,GHz, OpenSSL verify $14.7\,\mu$s, full pipeline $\overline{S} = 33.7\,\mu$s, $\mu = 29{,}689$\,fps). A single worker saturates under 100K\,pps flood ($\rho_1 = 3.37 > 1$), causing 138\,ms saturated queue wait and 100\% deadline miss. Deploying 4 workers scales throughput to $118{,}756$\,fps, maintaining stability at 100K\,pps ($\rho_4 = 0.84 < 1$) but failing when attack intensity reaches 500K\,pps ($\rho_4 = 4.21 \gg 1$).
\end{itemize}
Because substation IEDs operate within sealed, passively cooled enclosures with strict 1--2 core thermal limits, scaling processor cores cannot resolve CIL-DoS without pre-verification admission filtering.

\subsection{Hardware Scaling Analysis (Measured vs. Projected)}
Table~\ref{tab:hardware_scaling} models CIL-DoS vulnerability across five embedded hardware tiers. The benchmark host represents our measured platform (8-core ARMv8.5, 3.49\,GHz). The embedded relay tiers represent calibrated analytical projections derived by scaling the measured cryptographic service times using established ARM cycle counts and clock frequency ratios from published processor reference manuals~\cite{arm2020cortex, arm_cortex_r5, arm_cortex_m4, ali2020latency}. The $17.0\times$ scaling between the M2 host ($33.7\,\mu$s) and Cortex-A9 ($573\,\mu$s) accounts for both the $4.36\times$ clock ratio ($3.49\,\text{GHz}/0.8\,\text{GHz}$) and a $3.9\times$ microarchitectural IPC factor (wide out-of-order 64-bit pipeline vs.\ 32-bit dual-issue in-order core without 64-bit integer multipliers). Even high-performance relays saturate at just 1,745\,pps, while Cortex-M4 units saturate at 99\,pps.

