\subsection{PQAC Component Ablation Study and Analysis}
To dissect the individual contributions of each PQAC stage, we conducted a 5-configuration ablation study at 100K\,pps RSA-2048 attack (Table~\ref{tab:ablation_pqc}, Top):
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,noitemsep,topsep=1pt]
    \item \textbf{The Drop Mechanism Prevents Queue Collapse:} Comparing ABL-05 (2,318\,ms latency, 100\% miss) with ABL-01 through ABL-04 (all $\le 658\,\mu$s, 0.00\% steady-state miss) reveals that dropping 99\% of flood traffic at admission is the single most critical factor keeping queue utilization $\rho < 1$.
    \item \textbf{The Operational Necessity of All Components:} In our synthetic benchmark with a single unknown-MAC attacker, ABL-02 (No MAC), ABL-03 (No Rate), and ABL-04 (No Priority) converge to zero deadline miss in steady state because all attack packets share one unknown MAC. However, in an operational substation:
    \begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,noitemsep,topsep=1pt]
        \item \emph{MAC Allowlisting (ABL-02):} Without MAC verification, high-frequency legitimate fault bursts from authorized relays would be treated as unknown floods and dropped.
        \item \emph{Rate Tracking (ABL-03):} Without rate tracking, if an attacker spoofs an authorized publisher's MAC (Section~\ref{sec:spoof_eval}), all attack frames would enter HIGH, completely disabling the defense.
        \item \emph{Priority Scheduling (ABL-04):} Without priority differentiation, in multi-publisher substations with simultaneous fault bursts, admitted flood frames interleave ahead of trip commands in FIFO order, violating deterministic timing.
    \end{enumerate}
\end{itemize}

