\subsection{Post-Quantum Cryptography (PQC) Evaluation}
We evaluated NIST PQC standards (Table~\ref{tab:ablation_pqc}, Bottom) across standalone operations and network flood conditions:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,noitemsep,topsep=1pt]
    \item \textbf{SLH-DSA Inviability (FIPS 205):} Our empirical benchmarks reveal that subscriber verification for SLH-DSA-SHA2-128s executes in $260.6\,\mu$s ($\mu = 3{,}837$\,fps), which fits within the subscriber processing budget. However, SLH-DSA is fundamentally unviable for IEC~61850 real-time tripping due to two insurmountable bottlenecks: (i)~\emph{Publisher Signing Latency:} generating an SLH-DSA signature requires $295.4\text{--}597.4$\,ms, violating the 3\,ms trip deadline at the source by nearly two orders of magnitude before transmission; and (ii)~\emph{Jumbo Frame Requirement:} the 7,856-byte signature exceeds the standard 1,500-byte Ethernet MTU, requiring IEEE 802.3 jumbo frames (MTU 9,000) that standard substation process bus switches do not support.
    \item \textbf{Full Mathematical Verification Under Lattice Attacks:} When attack frames carry structurally valid signatures with bit-flipped digests, verifiers must execute complete NTT lattice mathematics (55.9\,$\mu$s for ML-DSA-44, 139.5\,$\mu$s for ML-DSA-87, and 24.0\,$\mu$s for FALCON-512). Under pure FIFO scheduling, a 100K\,pps attack saturates the queue ($\rho = 2.4\text{--}14.0 \gg 1$), causing 100\% deadline failure (latencies of 98.3--571.4\,ms). 
    \item \textbf{PQAC Shielding on Lattice Signatures:} PQAC drops 99\% of invalid flood frames at Stage~3 admission in $<0.2\,\mu$s. Prioritizing legitimate protection frames into Class~$H$ maintains queue utilization $\rho_H < 0.01$, achieving \textbf{0.00\% deadline violations} for ML-DSA-44 (P99 = 124.0\,$\mu$s) and FALCON-512 (P99 = 64.7\,$\mu$s).
    \item \textbf{Ethernet MTU and Security Levels:} ML-DSA-44 signatures (2,420 bytes) exceed the 1,500-byte Ethernet MTU, necessitating IEEE 802.3 jumbo frames (MTU 9,000) on substation switches. In contrast, FALCON-512 signatures (666 bytes) fit within standard Ethernet frames. ML-DSA-87 (Level~5 security) verifies in $139.5\,\mu$s, providing robust post-quantum security under PQAC protection with 0.00\% deadline miss.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Threshold Sensitivity, Scalability, and MAC Spoofing}
\label{sec:spoof_eval}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,noitemsep,topsep=1pt]
    \item \textbf{Threshold Sensitivity ($T_{\text{flood}}$):} Varying $T_{\text{flood}}$ from 20 to 2,000\,pps under 100K\,pps RSA attack confirmed that all settings maintain $<6\%$ deadline miss rates (Table~\ref{tab:sensitivity_scalability}). Settings $T_{\text{flood}} \ge 500$ achieve stable $<2\%$ miss rates without false positive classification on legitimate bursts.
    \item \textbf{Multi-Publisher Scalability:} Increasing active publishers from 1 to 16 increased deadline miss rate by only 0.40\% (from 1.75\% to 2.15\%). Because legitimate frames are classified as HIGH, they experience an effective utilization $\rho_{\text{legit}} = 16/1,745 \approx 0.009$, remaining isolated from flood traffic. In each trial, publishers emit continuous heartbeats alongside periodic fault trip burst sequences ($>100$ retransmissions per event, totaling $\approx 1{,}200$ frames per 60\,s trial and $>10{,}000$ across 10 repetitions), yielding fine-grained empirical miss statistics (e.g., 21 misses out of 1,200 frames $\approx 1.75\%$) rather than discrete 60-frame increments.
    \item \textbf{MAC Spoofing Dichotomy:} At 100K\,pps, an attacker spoofing legitimate publisher MACs is flagged as FLOOD within milliseconds by the sliding-window tracker, limiting miss rate to 0.87\%. However, at 10K\,pps, spoofed frames enter NORMAL, producing 77\% miss rate and proving that application-layer PQAC must be coupled with Layer~1 eBPF/XDP filtering.
    \item \textbf{The Warm-Up Transient:} As depicted in Fig.~\ref{fig:robustness}c, per-frame latency during initial attack onset spikes briefly while the tracker accumulates samples, converging within $\sim$0.5\,s to sub-1.5\,ms steady state.
\end{itemize}

