%% ================================================================
%%  SECTION IX: OPERATIONAL GUIDELINES FOR PROTECTION ENGINEERS
%% ================================================================
\section{Operational Guidelines for Substation Protection Engineers}
\label{sec:guidelines}
We recommend four deployment specifications for utility practice:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,noitemsep,topsep=1pt]
    \item \textbf{Pre-Verification Threshold Provisioning:} In SCL/CID files, configure admission thresholds aligned with bay topologies: $T_{\text{normal}} = 50$\,pps (heartbeats), $T_{\text{burst}} = 500$\,pps (retransmissions), and $T_{\text{flood}} = 1{,}000$\,pps.
    \item \textbf{Buffer Sizing and Anti-Bloat Policy:} Set verification queue depth to $K \le 512$ frames. Excessively deep buffers inflate latency during saturation ($W_{\text{sat}} = K \cdot \overline{S}$) without enhancing throughput.
    \item \textbf{PQC Migration Path:} Deploy lattice-based ML-DSA-44 or FALCON-512 alongside PQAC. Reject hash-based SLH-DSA (FIPS 205) for time-critical GOOSE circuits. Ensure switches support IEEE 802.3 jumbo frames (MTU 9,000) prior to ML-DSA roll-out.
    \item \textbf{Perimeter Reinforcement:} Pair PQAC with IEEE 802.1X port security~\cite{ieee8021x} and MACsec (IEEE 802.1AE) or switch port security to suppress unauthorized process bus injection at the physical switch boundary.
\end{enumerate}

%% ================================================================
%%  SECTION X: LIMITATIONS AND THREATS TO VALIDITY
%% ================================================================
\balance
\section{Limitations and Threats to Validity}
\label{sec:limitations}
\textbf{Hardware Realism:} While models evaluate five processor tiers, empirical benchmarks ran on an 8-core ARMv8.5 host rather than utility-grade IEDs (SEL/GE relays). RTOS validation is planned.
\textbf{Timing Determinism and WCET:} Linux container namespaces introduce minor scheduling jitter compared to hard real-time operating systems (RTOS like FreeRTOS or VxWorks). Formal safety certification of protective relays requires worst-case execution time (WCET) analysis with static upper bounds. However, 2,318\,ms queueing delays dwarf microsecond scheduling jitter.
\textbf{Layer~2 MAC Spoofing Boundary:} Ethernet Layer~2 MAC spoofing is fundamentally an architectural characteristic of switched Ethernet LANs. While PQAC incorporates verify-before-commit and transition token gating to isolate spoofed bursts, defense against high-rate spoofed traffic requires coupling application-layer PQAC with Layer~2 port security (IEEE 802.1X~\cite{ieee8021x}, MACsec, or switch port security).
\textbf{Network Architecture:} Our testbed models a single process bus; PRP/HSR redundant architectures (IEC 62439-3) require coordinated multi-interface admission control.

%% ================================================================
%%  SECTION XI: CONCLUSION
%% ================================================================
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
This paper demonstrates that cryptographic authentication in IEC~62351-6 introduces an exploitable vulnerability---Crypto-Induced Latency DoS (CIL-DoS)---that saturates IED verification queues and delays GOOSE trip commands by 2,318\,ms on embedded relay profiles (138\,ms on high-speed hosts), violating the 3\,ms safety deadline, exceeding Critical Clearing Time ($t_{\text{crit}} \approx 201.6$\,ms base, $\approx 120$\,ms in low-inertia grids), and triggering a 6.25$\times$ surge in transformer $I^2 t$ thermal energy under delayed backup clearance. We proved that buffering and parallel threads cannot resolve this saturation. We developed the Priority Queue Admission Controller (PQAC), proved its queue stability, and demonstrated up to 2,110$\times$ latency reduction on measured testbed hardware (1,937$\times$ on embedded relay profiles) across 49 controlled experiments, achieving 0.00\% deadline miss under pre-provisioned allowlists. Furthermore, our evaluation of NIST PQC standards established that SLH-DSA is fundamentally unusable in real-time substations, whereas ML-DSA-44 and FALCON-512 achieve robust performance under PQAC protection. We recommend that future revisions of IEC~62351-6 incorporate mandatory pre-verification admission control for all asymmetric and post-quantum substation deployments.

